{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310609,"nonce":"tR9T13n2Lp97ZMlzjko2C2UWxZo=","title":"Frozen archive of critical bug fixes 2026-02-13 to 2026-03-09 (class allocation size mismatch, blade cache stale refs, @:forward abstract ...","message":"This archive freezes the \"Critical Bugs Fixed (Recent)\" section that used\nto live inline in MEMORY.md. Each entry kept its original\n_problem / root cause / fix / key files / key insight_ structure.\nNone of these bugs have regressed; the fixes are load-bearing in the\ncurrent compiler.\n\n**Problem**: Intermittent SIGABRT in `haxe_string_concat_impl` when running `test_exception_method_parity`. Exception object allocated 16 bytes instead of needed 32 bytes.\n**Root cause**: `hir_to_mir.rs` line ~15448 fallback used `args.len()` (constructor arg count) for allocation size when `class_alloc_sizes` lookup failed due to TypeId instability across compilation contexts. `new Exception(\"boom\")` → 1 arg → 16 bytes, but Exception has 4 fields (type_id+message+previous+stack) = 32 bytes. Writing slots 2-3 corrupted adjacent heap memory.\n**Fix**: Added `class_alloc_sizes_by_name: BTreeMap\u003cString, u64\u003e` — name-based lookup stable across contexts. Populated in `register_class_metadata`, seeded from imports in `compilation.rs`, passed as parameter to `lower_hir_to_mir_with_function_map`. Also stored in `MirLoweringResult` for cross-file accumulation. BLADE cache also populates name-based sizes.\n**Key files**: `hir_to_mir.rs` (name-based lookup chain), `compilation.rs` (accumulation + passing).\n\n**Problem**: COMPILE_FAIL for `next` functions in cached runs of `test_stringbuf_stringtools`. `Called function IrFunctionId(120018) not found in module`.\n**Root cause**: Cached MIR modules embed cross-module function IDs from the original compilation session. When modules get different base offsets on reload, those references become stale. E.g., StringIterator.next calls StringTools.unsafeCodeAt at ID 120018, but in new session StringTools is renumbered to 110000+ base.\n**Fix**: (1) Added `external_function_names: BTreeMap\u003cIrFunctionId, String\u003e` to `IrModule` (with `#[serde(default)]`). Populated at end of MIR lowering by scanning module for CallDirect refs to functions not in the module's own functions/externs, mapped via `external_function_name_map`. (2) During `renumber_and_push_import_mir`, resolve stale refs via name lookup in `stdlib_function_name_map`. (3) Added `fixup_stale_cross_module_refs()` post-pass after ALL imports loaded, to handle cases where target module wasn't loaded yet during renumbering.\n**Key files**: `modules.rs` (IrModule field), `hir_to_mir.rs` (populate external names), `compilation.rs` (renumber resolution + fixup pass).\n\n**Problem**: `safe.pop()` on `@:forward(pop) abstract SafeArray(Array\u003cInt\u003e)` crashed. Array pointer was boxed via `haxe_box_int_ptr` before being passed to `array_pop` MIR wrapper.\n**Root cause**: Two issues: (1) `is_type_erased_ptr` check in MIR wrapper arg processing (~line 9643) didn't include `TypeKind::Abstract`, so abstract-typed args with `I64` register type were boxed instead of cast. (2) `build_alloc` doesn't set register type, so `get_register_type()` returns `None` → fallback `I64` → triggers integer boxing path.\n**Fix**: Added `TypeKind::Abstract { .. }` to the `is_type_erased_ptr` match list. The `@:forward` stdlib dispatch via `get_stdlib_runtime_info` was already working correctly (resolves through underlying type).\n\n**Problem**: Cross-function throw+catch crashed with SIGILL. `thrower()` called with 1 arg (class type_id 231) instead of 0 args.\n**Root cause**: `tast_to_hir.rs` changed `StaticMethodCall` lowering from `Variable(method_symbol)` to `Field(Variable(class_symbol), method_symbol)`. MIR lowerer's Field callee path always passes object as `this`.\n**Fix**: Added `is_static_class_call` detection in hir_to_mir.rs (~line 7809). Check if object is a class/abstract/typealias symbol via `SymbolKind`, and if so, handle as static call without passing object as `this`.\n\n**Problem**: `var d:Dynamic = new Point(3, 7); d.sum()` returned 0. Dynamic method calls dispatched to wrong function or passed boxed pointer instead of raw object pointer.\n**Root Causes**: Three bugs:\n1. **Stdlib name collision**: `is_stdlib_method` returned TRUE for user methods like `sum` because SIMD4f/Tensor also have `sum`. Dynamic user method calls dispatched to stdlib instead of user functions.\n2. **Missing unbox in Field callee path**: When `get_function_id(field)` succeeds but receiver is Dynamic, raw boxed DynamicValue pointer passed as `this` instead of unboxed object pointer.\n3. **Ptr(Void) vs Ptr(U8) unbox check**: `should_unbox` required `Ptr(U8)` register type, but Dynamic values are cast from `Ptr(U8)` to `Ptr(Void)` during lowering, so unbox was skipped.\n**Fix** (hir_to_mir.rs, 3 locations):\n1. **User method priority** (~line 7749): Before `is_stdlib_method` check, search `function_map` for user-defined methods when receiver is Dynamic. If found, unbox and call directly.\n2. **Field callee unbox** (~line 6030): After `lower_expression(object)`, check if object.ty is Dynamic and unbox via `haxe_unbox_reference_ptr`.\n3. **should_unbox fix** (~line 8147): Changed from `Ptr(U8)` check to `!has_class_hint` — Dynamic receivers are always boxed.\n**Key insight**: Dynamic receivers are ALWAYS boxed (DynamicValue wrapping class pointer). Must unbox at EVERY call site that dispatches to a class method, regardless of register type being Ptr(Void) or Ptr(U8).\n\n**Problem**: `palette[i].r + palette[i].g` crashed. `_ =\u003e true` fallback assumed non-Variable expressions produce boxed DynamicValues. Actually class field access produces raw i32/f64.\n**Fix**: Check `builder.get_register_type()` — concrete types (I32, I64, F64, Bool) are NOT boxed.\n**Key insight**: HIR type says Dynamic, but MIR register type reveals the truth.\n\n**Problem**: `debug_checksum.hx` crashed — phi-defined registers had no entry in `register_types`.\n**Fix**: Phi node type lookup as fallback in cranelift_backend.rs Cmp handler.\n\n**Problem**: Map for-in crashed with SIGABRT. `Alloc` (stack slot) treated as heap allocation.\n**Fix**: Removed `Alloc` from InsertFreePass scanning. Only `malloc`/`rayzor_anon_new` need explicit freeing.\n\n**Problem**: mandelbrot crashed — malloc in loop body, InsertFreePass assumed allocs dominate return block.\n**Fix**: Partition entry-block vs inner-block allocs. Inner-block freed at last-use block.\n\n**Problem**: `next_reg_id` stale after inlining, SRA allocated colliding register IDs.\n**Fix**: Recompute `next_reg_id` at start of `apply_sra`.\n\n**Problem**: `node = node.right` in while loop caused SIGABRT. Drop tracking treated loaded field pointers as owned allocations.\n**Root Cause**: Assign handler used `rhs_type_needs_drop` (true for any class type) to trigger `register_owned_value`, even for field access (borrowed reference). On next loop iteration, `register_owned_value` freed the old value — a borrowed pointer into an existing tree node.\n**Fix**: Only call `register_owned_value` when RHS is `New` or `Call` (actual allocations). For `lhs_was_tracked` + borrowed RHS: free old, stop tracking. For untracked + field access RHS: no action.\n**Key insight**: `rhs_type_needs_drop` alone is too broad. Must also check RHS expression kind — field access/variable reads produce borrowed references, only New/Call produce owned allocations.\n\n**Problem**: `this.field` implicit access crashed in `lower_constructor` — `SymbolId::from_raw(0)` not in `symbol_map`.\n**Fix**: Add `self.symbol_map.insert(SymbolId::from_raw(0), this_reg)` in `lower_constructor` (was already in `lower_constructor_body`).\n\n**Problem**: Calling functions with fewer args than params crashed with CALL MISMATCH. Default values existed in TAST/HIR but were lost at MIR call sites.\n**Fix**: Store `function_param_defaults: BTreeMap\u003cIrFunctionId, Vec\u003cOption\u003cHirExpr\u003e\u003e\u003e` during Pass 1 registration. `fill_default_args()` helper lowers missing defaults at 4 call sites (constructor, super, method, static).\n\n**Problem**: `gep i32 ptr, [3]` computed 3*4=12 instead of 3*8=24. All Rayzor field slots are 8 bytes but GEP used `sizeof(elem_type)`.\n**Fix**: Cranelift backend GEP handler uses `elem_size = 8` for struct field GEPs. Was masked by SRA for simple classes.\n**REFINED**: `elem_size = 8` unconditionally broke byte-pointer GEPs (`gep *u8 ptr, [offset]`) introduced by O2 optimization passes. Byte offsets got multiplied by 8 → SIGBUS. Fix: check `ty` — if `Ptr(U8)` or `Ptr(I8)`, use `elem_size = 1`.\n**Key insight**: O2 optimization introduces `*u8`-typed GEPs (from PtrAdd lowering) not present at O0. The unconditional `elem_size = 8` only worked because `rayzor run` uses O0.\n\n**Problem**: `Body.Jupiter()` in multi-class files produced no MIR output. The call-site symbol (SymbolId X) had no qualified name, while the definition symbol (SymbolId Y) had `Body.Jupiter` as qualified name.\n**Root Cause**: `get_function_id(symbol)` returned None because symbol IDs differ. The qualified-name fallback also failed because the call-site symbol had `qualified_name = None`.\n**Fix**: Added bare-name fallback in `hir_to_mir.rs` (~line 10450): when the call-site symbol has no qualified name, search `function_map` and `external_function_map` by bare function name.\n**Key insight**: Multi-class files create separate SymbolIds for call-site references vs definition-site symbols, and the call-site may lack a qualified name.\n\n**Problem**: `case MkPair(Some(a), Some(b)):` crashed when scrutinee was `MkPair(Some(10), None)`. Three root causes:\n1. **OOB field read**: Tag-only enum variants (`None`) are 8-byte allocations. Pattern test for `Some(b)` unconditionally loaded field at offset 8 even when tag didn't match → OOB read → SIGSEGV.\n2. **Wrong parent enum**: `find_parent_enum_for_constructor(None_symbol)` returned `DisplayMode` (stdlib) instead of user's `Option` because both have `None` variant. Fix: use `resolve_enum_symbol(expr.ty)` to get correct parent from the expression's type.\n3. **SymbolId mismatch across generics**: Variant SymbolIds differ between generic instantiation and definition. Variant loop `*variant_id == *symbol` never matched. Fix: fall back to name-based matching.\n**Key insight — build_phi must set_register_type**: `build_phi()` didn't call `set_register_type()`, causing downstream BinOp/Cmp handlers to fail with \"Type not found\".\n**Key insight — short-circuit pattern fields**: When testing a Constructor pattern with fields on a boxed enum, must branch on tag match BEFORE loading fields. Other variants may have smaller allocations.\n\n**What**: `new haxe.ds.BalancedTree\u003cInt, String\u003e()` now auto-imports without explicit `import haxe.ds.BalancedTree;`.\n**Key file**: `compilation.rs` — `collect_qualified_type_refs_from_ast()` walks the parsed AST collecting all `TypePath` with non-empty packages.\n**Root cause of original bug**: Only explicit `import` statements triggered `load_imports_efficiently()`. Fully-qualified type names in code were silently ignored → stdlib file never loaded → all method calls silently dropped (no MIR generated).\n**Key insight — ext_map_size=0**: When stdlib class methods are missing, `external_function_map` is empty. The stdlib file was never compiled. Adding qualified type refs to the import list before `load_imports_efficiently()` fixes it.\n\n**What**: `haxe.ds.EnumValueMap` — enum-keyed map using BalancedTree + virtual dispatch for `compare()`.\n**Key insight — type constraint validation**: `K:EnumValue` constraint validation already relaxed for abstract types in `type_checking_pipeline.rs`.\n**Key insight — Reflect.compare NOT FOUND**: SymbolId(130) for `Reflect.compare` not found during BalancedTree's separate compilation, but the stdlib mapping fallback handles it correctly.\n**Test**: `set(Color.Red, \"red\")` → `get(Color.Red)` returns `\"red\"`, `exists(Color.Red)` returns true.\n\nSee `bugs_history.md` for details on: SIMD4f dispatch, @:op overloading, Dynamic anon field access, vector replace_uses, SIMD4f @:from, lambda/closure, SRA stale index, nested generics, anon shapes, stdlib merge, static method stubs, implicit `this`, array ops, InsertFree BitCast, LLVM ICmp, Int-\u003eFloat coercion, FMA+SRA.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_fixed_2026_02_03_archive` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}